# #107 — HOW A BINARY ASSET LEAVES THE CONTROL-BYTE SWEEP.
#
# test/source-control-bytes.test.js reads every file git tracks and fails on a raw U+0000,
# because that byte hides a source file from grep, rg and git grep while Node reads it fine.
# A PNG is very largely raw NULs, so the day one is committed the guard goes permanently red.
#
# The sweep's scope is THIS FILE, and deliberately not a content sniff. git's own binary
# heuristic reads the first 8000 bytes for a NUL — which is exactly the byte under guard, so
# sniffing would let a .js file with a NUL near the top classify itself out of the sweep and
# vanish, the precise blind spot #107 exists to prevent. A line here cannot do that quietly:
# it is reviewable, it names the kind out loud, and test/source-control-bytes.qa.test.js
# fails if a source extension ever appears below, or if a tracked file git considers binary
# is NOT declared here.
#
# So: adding an asset kind is a decision someone makes on the record, which is what the
# retired extension-scoped sweep was groping for.

*.png binary
